## Context

Move cleanup after replacement paths work. This ticket now owns staged rollout and removal of obsolete code/prose, not an independent harness-tier budget or early destructive cleanup.

Design baseline: v7. v6 was merged by PR #903 and is frozen at `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-v6.md`; v7 closes the acceptance review's four §12.1 conditions and settles the nomenclature, leaving the architecture and this ticket's scope unchanged. See `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md`, `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-nomenclature.md` and `docs/2026-09-11-memory-implementation-plan.md`. This planning change implements nothing and closes no work.

## Relevant files

Existing `projects/*/memory/`, shared/IDH roots; legacy memory/dream skills and readers; resident budget tests; validated pilot migration tooling.

## Actions

1. Before broad activation or retiring a working path, inspect 0918 results against the predeclared acceptance margins. Unmet margins require remediation or an explicitly recorded scope/risk decision; a green mechanical smoke alone is insufficient.
2. Inventory each target project and audience; batch migration using the proven identity/date mapping. Keep unrelated external repositories behind their own normal PR processes.
3. Record each batch and language/provenance exceptions. Do not automatically translate quotations or copy private data into a shared repository.
4. Retire legacy decay/TTL/confirmation, duplicate index writers and obsolete promotion rules only after their consumers have migrated.
5. Replace discovery with the common loader/knowledge-hint path and check no legacy reader treats an index as canonical storage.

## Test

Replay pilot migration on representative batch fixtures; compare identity/source coverage and relative links. Prove replacement consumer works before removing its old path; exercise a deliberately missed consumer.

## Verification

- [ ] Run the focused fixtures and show they detect the relevant failure.
- [ ] Run applicable repository checks; record commands, results and limitations.
- [ ] Confirm the implementation matches this scope and v7, including explicit capability limits.

## Invariants

- Canonical bodies and provenance remain reviewable; memory cannot override decisions.
- No mandatory residency, automatic generalisation, new native-memory modes or policy duplication.
- Use the common library and normal Git integration; no runtime implementation is part of the planning PR.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Rollout batches and exceptions recorded with reversible mappings.
- [ ] No two canonical writers or automatic destructive promotion remain.
- [ ] Old machinery retired only where replacement coverage is verified; no ticket closes on grep emptiness alone.
